;; A membership extract on a variable-length dataset: one record type, every
;; record a member, and inside each member a counted table of addresses whose
;; entries carry roles rather than types.
;;
;; What is hard about it, and why it is a worked example of its own, is in
;; README.md beside this file. In short: the REDEFINES in `member.cpy` is inside
;; a group that repeats, so it stays a variant rather than resolving into record
;; types -- and nothing in an entry's bytes says which alternative it is, so
;; there is nothing for a predicate to test. Where the entry sits decides, and
;; this file is where that is written.

;; The encoding profile. All four axes, always, with no default for any.
(encoding
  (charset cp037)
  (sign-convention ebcdic)
  (byte-order big-endian)
  (float-format ieee-754))

;; The dataset. VB rather than FB, and not a free choice: the reading below
;; makes a member's extent move with the number of addresses they gave, and a
;; variable-extent record type is refused on a fixed-length dataset -- see
;; docs/layout/SPEC.md, "`lrecl` and `blksize` describe the dataset, not the
;; stream". The framing follows the reading; it does not follow the schedule,
;; which is the point the comment on `copybook-reading` below makes.
(framing
  (recfm VB)
  (lrecl 512))

;; The record type. One, because this file is a flat extract of members with no
;; header and no trailer, and because what the example is here to show happens
;; inside a record rather than between records -- `ledger/` is the example of a
;; file whose record types have to be told apart, and `policy/` of a file that
;; has eleven of them.
(record MEMBER-RECORD (copybook "member.cpy" MEMBER-RECORD))

;; How `MBR-ADDRESS OCCURS 1 TO 3 TIMES DEPENDING ON MBR-ADDRESS-COUNT` is read,
;; and the one statement in this layout that no copybook could have made: it is
;; a property of the compiler that wrote the file, and both readings are
;; ordinary.
;;
;; `odoslide`, because the file this describes is one IBM Enterprise COBOL
;; wrote, which slides unconditionally and has no directive to name. Under it
;; the table is a repetition whose count is read out of MBR-ADDRESS-COUNT, and
;; MBR-STATUS begins sixty-one bytes on from wherever the last entry ended -- a
;; different offset in a member with one address and a member with three.
;;
;; `noodoslide` would describe a different file out of the same copybook: three
;; entries always present, MBR-ADDRESS-COUNT an ordinary field beside them
;; saying how many are meant, and every member the same 225 bytes. Nothing in
;; the bytes disagrees with the wrong reading, which is why there is no default
;; and why this form is required rather than inferred.
;;
;; The schedule below is legal under either, and the text of it would not
;; change. Which reading applies is a property of the extract rather than of the
;; copybook, so it must not also decide whether the copybook can be described --
;; docs/layout/SPEC.md, "It is available whichever way the table's count is
;; read". What the reading changes is how many of the scheduled arms a given
;; record reaches, not which occurrence takes which arm.
(copybook-reading
  (occurs-depending-on odoslide))

;; What selects the record type. Nothing does: every record in the extract is a
;; member, so there is nothing for a predicate to test and `single-record-type`
;; says so. Writing it is what makes that a statement rather than a gap --
;; every `record` form is named by exactly one `discriminate`, this one
;; included.
(discriminate MEMBER-RECORD single-record-type)

;; And the variant. `ADR-HOME` is described three ways inside a group that
;; repeats, so the choice is made once per entry rather than once per record.
;; A `schedule-variant` rather than a `discriminate-variant`, because no byte of
;; an entry says which of the three it carries: the enrolment screen asks for a
;; home address, then a work address, then a mailing address, and the order they
;; were written in is the whole of the rule. An arm here names occurrences
;; rather than carrying a predicate, and a variant is scheduled or discriminated
;; and never both.
;;
;; The argument is the redefined item, exactly as `discriminate-variant`'s is --
;; `ADR-HOME` is the first alternative over those bytes and not a declaration
;; standing in front of the real ones, so it is both the variant and an arm.
;;
;; The occurrence numbers count from one and the schedule covers the table
;; exactly once from one to its declared maximum of three; `resolve` rejects one
;; that leaves an entry uncovered, naming the occurrences with no arm. That
;; check is static, which is why an entry matching no arm is not a failure a
;; reader of this file can meet -- the one thing a schedule takes away from the
;; byte-selected form, and what it gives back for a file that carries no byte to
;; select on.
;;
;; The roles are named rather than numbered, and that is what makes the three
;; lines below read as the schedule the enrolment screen enforces rather than as
;; arithmetic over a table.
(schedule-variant (item MEMBER-RECORD MBR-ADDRESS ADR-HOME)
  (arm ADR-HOME 1)
  (arm ADR-WORK 2)
  (arm ADR-MAIL 3))

;; The order they may appear in. One or more members and nothing else, so `+`
;; rather than `*`: an extract of no members is not a file this describes.
(sequence
  (+ MEMBER-RECORD))
